(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-anatomy-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"Anatomy Exam 4 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form D","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which foramen is formed by the ischium and pubis together, on the lateral aspect of the coxal bone?","choices":["Obturator foramen","Iliac fossa","Acetabulum","Greater sciatic notch"],"answer":0,"exp":"The obturator foramen is the large opening formed jointly by the ischium and pubis on the coxal bone. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which proximal fibular landmark articulates with the tibia at the superior tibiofibular joint?","choices":["Shaft of the fibula","Interosseous border","Head of the fibula","Lateral malleolus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The head of the fibula is the proximal landmark that articulates with the tibia at the superior tibiofibular joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which scapular structure directly receives the head of the humerus?","choices":["The acromion","The subscapular fossa","The glenoid cavity","The scapular notch"],"answer":2,"exp":"The glenoid cavity (fossa) is the shallow lateral socket of the scapula that articulates with the head of the humerus at the glenohumeral joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"How many metacarpal bones are in each hand?","choices":["5","14","4","8"],"answer":0,"exp":"There are 5 metacarpal bones in each hand, numbered I to V. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which structure connects the shafts of the radius and ulna along their length?","choices":["The interosseous membrane","The anular ligament","The joint capsule of the elbow","The intertubercular sulcus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The interosseous membrane is the fibrous sheet that connects the interosseous borders of the radial and ulnar shafts along their length. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which tarsal bone articulates superiorly with the tibia and fibula to help form the ankle joint?","choices":["The calcaneus","The cuboid","The cuneiform","The talus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The talus is the tarsal bone that articulates with the tibia and fibula superiorly, forming the ankle (talocrural) joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"What is the deltoid tuberosity, and where is it located?","choices":["A roughened area on the humeral shaft for deltoid muscle attachment","A proximal articular surface where the humeral head meets the scapula","A distal landmark that helps form the joint with the radial head","A groove on the posterior humerus that carries the ulnar nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"The deltoid tuberosity is a roughened area on the shaft (body) of the humerus, serving as the attachment site for the deltoid muscle. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which two bones form the pectoral (shoulder) girdle on each side?","choices":["The humerus and the scapula","The clavicle and the scapula","The clavicle and the sternum","The scapula and the first rib"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each pectoral girdle is formed by the clavicle anteriorly and the scapula posteriorly, connecting the upper limb to the axial skeleton. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which anterior projection of the proximal ulna forms the lower part of the trochlear notch?","choices":["The olecranon","The coronoid process","The styloid process","The radial tuberosity"],"answer":1,"exp":"The coronoid process is the anterior projection of the proximal ulna that forms the inferior margin of the trochlear notch and fits into the coronoid fossa in flexion. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which tarsal bone forms the heel and is the largest bone of the foot?","choices":["The talus","The navicular","The calcaneus","The cuboid"],"answer":2,"exp":"The calcaneus is the largest tarsal bone and forms the heel of the foot, transmitting body weight to the ground. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"How many tarsal bones are in each foot?","choices":["8 tarsal bones","7 tarsal bones","5 tarsal bones","14 tarsal bones"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each foot has 7 tarsal bones forming the posterior half of the foot, including the talus and calcaneus. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"What is the tibial tuberosity, and where is it located?","choices":["A proximal anterior landmark for patellar ligament attachment","A distal landmark that forms the medial malleolus of the ankle","A distal landmark located on the fibula, not the tibia","A proximal posterior ridge that serves as a muscle attachment site"],"answer":0,"exp":"The tibial tuberosity is a proximal, anterior landmark on the tibia that serves as the attachment site for the patellar ligament. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which end of the clavicle is the acromial end?","choices":["The lateral end","The medial end","The anterior end","The posterior end"],"answer":0,"exp":"The acromial end of the clavicle is its lateral end, articulating with the acromion of the scapula. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the iliac fossa?","choices":["A bony ridge running along the pelvic brim margin","The rough outer (lateral) surface of the ilium","The smooth, concave inner (medial) surface of the ilium","The deep socket that receives the femoral head"],"answer":2,"exp":"The iliac fossa is the smooth, concave medial (internal) surface of the ilium, providing origin for the iliacus muscle. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"With which part of the sternum does the sternal end of the clavicle articulate?","choices":["The manubrium of the sternum","The xiphoid process below","The body of the sternum","The jugular notch margin"],"answer":0,"exp":"The medial (sternal) end of the clavicle articulates with the manubrium of the sternum at the sternoclavicular joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"What distinguishes a primary spinal curve from a secondary spinal curve?","choices":["Primary curves occur only in the cervical region; secondary only in the lumbar region","Primary curves develop after birth; secondary curves develop before birth","Primary curves develop before birth; secondary curves develop after birth","There is no developmental difference between them"],"answer":2,"exp":"Primary curves develop before birth, while secondary curves develop after birth (such as the cervical curve developing as an infant learns to balance its head, and the lumbar curve developing with the ability to stand). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What is the sella turcica, and what does it house?","choices":["A ridge on the ethmoid bone that anchors the nasal septum superiorly","A bony process of the temporal bone that helps form the zygomatic arch","A paired foramen in the sphenoid transmitting branches of the trigeminal nerve","A saddle-shaped depression on the sphenoid body that houses the pituitary gland (hypophyseal fossa)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The sella turcica is a saddle-shaped depression on the body of the sphenoid, containing the hypophyseal fossa that houses the pituitary gland. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which large opening in the occipital bone transmits the spinal cord as it joins the brainstem?","choices":["The optic canal","The jugular foramen","The mental foramen","The foramen magnum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The foramen magnum is the large opening in the occipital bone through which the spinal cord passes to join the brainstem. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What are the two structural functions of the thoracic cage listed in the material?","choices":["Forming the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone and enclosing the pituitary gland","Producing red blood cells within its marrow and storing the body's calcium reserves","Supporting the skull directly and transferring the body's weight down to the lower limbs","Protecting thoracic organs and serving as an attachment site for respiration/posture/limb-movement muscles"],"answer":3,"exp":"The thoracic cage protects the heart, lungs, thymus, and other structures within the cavity, and serves as an attachment site for muscles involved in respiration, positioning the vertebral column, and movements of the pectoral girdle and upper limb. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which unpaired facial bone forms the inferior and posterior part of the bony nasal septum?","choices":["The vomer bone","The nasal bone","The lacrimal bone","The zygomatic bone"],"answer":0,"exp":"The vomer is the thin, plow-shaped bone that forms the inferoposterior part of the bony nasal septum. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What three parts make up the sternum?","choices":["Manubrium, jugular notch, and body only","Manubrium, xiphoid process, and costal cartilage","Manubrium, body, and xiphoid process","Body, xiphoid process, and clavicular notch"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sternum consists of the manubrium, body, and xiphoid process. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"How many originally separate vertebrae fuse to form the adult sacrum?","choices":["Twelve","Three","Five","Seven"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sacrum consists of five fused vertebrae, though it exists as one bone in the adult. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which perforated ethmoid plate transmits the olfactory nerve fibers from the nasal cavity?","choices":["The perpendicular plate","The pterygoid plate","The cribriform plate","The horizontal plate"],"answer":2,"exp":"The cribriform plate of the ethmoid is perforated by numerous foramina that transmit olfactory nerve fibers from the nasal cavity into the cranium. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Along the inferior inner surface of a typical rib, which groove protects the intercostal vessels and nerve?","choices":["The intertubercular groove","The radial groove","The mylohyoid groove","The costal groove"],"answer":3,"exp":"The costal groove runs along the inferior internal margin of a typical rib and shelters the intercostal vein, artery, and nerve. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"The vertebral foramen of a typical vertebra transmits which structure?","choices":["The vertebral artery only","The spinal cord","A spinal nerve root only","The esophagus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vertebral foramen is the central opening through which the spinal cord passes; the stacked foramina form the vertebral canal. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which bone forms the floor of the orbit?","choices":["Frontal bone","Maxilla","Zygomatic bone","Sphenoid"],"answer":1,"exp":"The maxilla forms the floor of the orbit. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What structures make up the nasal septum?","choices":["The sphenoid bone together with the frontal bone","The paired nasal bones and the lacrimal bones","The vomer and the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid","The maxilla bone together with the palatine bone"],"answer":2,"exp":"The nasal septum is made up of the vomer (inferiorly) and the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid (superiorly). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which air-filled space, the largest of the paranasal sinuses, lies within the maxilla?","choices":["The frontal sinus","The sphenoidal sinus","The mastoid air cells","The maxillary sinus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The maxillary sinus is the largest paranasal sinus, occupying the body of the maxilla on each side. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"The maxillae form which part of the mouth and hold which set of teeth?","choices":["The upper jaw, holding the upper teeth","The lower jaw, holding the lower teeth","The hard palate only, holding no teeth","The cheekbones, holding no teeth"],"answer":0,"exp":"The maxillae fuse to form the upper jaw and hold the upper (maxillary) teeth in their alveolar processes. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which slender temporal-bone spike serves as an attachment point for muscles of the tongue and pharynx?","choices":["The mastoid process","The zygomatic arch","The crista galli","The styloid process"],"answer":3,"exp":"The styloid process is the thin pointed projection of the temporal bone that anchors several muscles and ligaments of the tongue and pharynx. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is the function of the deep fasciae separating limb muscles into compartments?","choices":["They are the primary structures responsible for transmitting electrical nerve signals directly into the muscle fibers","They form the dense fibrous tendons that anchor the ends of each skeletal muscle directly to the periosteum covering nearby bone","They form the periosteum, the fibrous membrane that covers and nourishes the surface of the underlying limb bones","They support and interconnect adjacent skeletal muscles while permitting independent movement, separating soft tissues into compartments"],"answer":3,"exp":"The connective tissue fibers of the deep fasciae support and interconnect adjacent skeletal muscles but permit independent movement, and the deep fascia separates the soft tissues of the limb into separate compartments. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"The muscles of the posterior forearm compartment (the extensors) are supplied mainly by which nerve?","choices":["The median nerve","The radial nerve","The ulnar nerve","The musculocutaneous nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The posterior forearm extensor compartment is supplied by the radial nerve and its deep branch. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which peripheral nerve injury is specifically associated with paralysis of the serratus anterior in the material?","choices":["Radial nerve lesion","Median nerve lesion (carpal tunnel syndrome)","Axillary nerve lesion","Long thoracic nerve lesion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Serratus anterior paralysis is typically due to dysfunction (a lesion) of the long thoracic nerve. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which muscle is the prime mover of elbow flexion and also supinates the forearm?","choices":["The triceps brachii","The pronator teres","The biceps brachii","The anconeus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The biceps brachii flexes the elbow and is a powerful supinator of the forearm, especially against resistance. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which muscle produces flexion, adduction, and medial rotation of the arm at the shoulder?","choices":["Deltoid muscle","Pectoralis major","Infraspinatus muscle","Teres minor muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Pectoralis major produces flexion, adduction, and medial rotation of the arm at the shoulder. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How do the dorsal interossei and palmar interossei differ in their actions on the fingers?","choices":["Dorsal interossei abduct digits 2-4; palmar interossei adduct digits 2, 4, and 5","Dorsal interossei flex the digits; palmar interossei extend the digits","Dorsal interossei adduct digits 2-4; palmar interossei abduct digits 2, 4, and 5","Both abduct the same digits"],"answer":0,"exp":"Dorsal interossei abduct digits 2 through 4, while palmar interossei adduct digits 2, 4, and 5. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"The iliopsoas is formed by the union of which two muscles?","choices":["The gluteus maximus and medius","The biceps femoris and gracilis","The psoas major and the iliacus","The soleus and gastrocnemius"],"answer":2,"exp":"The iliopsoas is formed by the psoas major, arising from the lumbar vertebrae, and the iliacus, arising from the iliac fossa. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which anterior leg muscle is the prime dorsiflexor of the foot at the ankle?","choices":["The gastrocnemius muscle","The soleus muscle","The tibialis anterior","The fibularis longus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The tibialis anterior in the anterior leg compartment is the prime dorsiflexor of the foot and also inverts it. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which muscle makes up the posterior compartment of the arm?","choices":["Biceps brachii","Brachialis","Triceps brachii","Coracobrachialis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The posterior compartment of the arm is made up of the triceps brachii. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which large tendon, formed by the calf muscles, is the strongest and thickest tendon in the body?","choices":["The calcaneal (Achilles) tendon","The patellar tendon of the knee","The biceps tendon of the arm","The quadriceps tendon above"],"answer":0,"exp":"The calcaneal, or Achilles, tendon formed by the gastrocnemius and soleus is the strongest and thickest tendon in the body. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Action lines that cross the anterior aspect of a joint classify a muscle as which type?","choices":["An abductor/lateral rotator","A flexor","An extensor","An adductor/medial rotator"],"answer":1,"exp":"Action lines that cross the anterior aspect of a joint classify a muscle as a flexor. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How is the skeletal musculature of the body subdivided, per the material?","choices":["Voluntary musculature and involuntary musculature","Axial musculature and appendicular musculature","Superficial musculature and deep musculature","Flexor musculature and extensor musculature"],"answer":1,"exp":"The skeletal muscle of the body is subdivided into axial musculature (positions the head/vertebral column and moves the rib cage) and appendicular musculature (stabilizes/moves the appendicular skeleton). — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which large posterior leg muscle produces plantar flexion and is notable for having its tendon become the calcaneal (Achilles) tendon along with the gastrocnemius?","choices":["Tibialis anterior","Soleus","Fibularis brevis","Extensor digitorum longus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Soleus produces plantar flexion of the foot, and together with the gastrocnemius, contributes to the calcaneal tendon. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which muscle is the prime mover for abduction of the arm at the shoulder past the first several degrees?","choices":["The supraspinatus","The deltoid muscle","The pectoralis major","The latissimus dorsi"],"answer":1,"exp":"The deltoid, especially its middle fibers, is the prime mover for abduction of the arm at the glenohumeral joint. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which nerve supplies the gluteus maximus, the main thigh extensor?","choices":["The superior gluteal nerve","The inferior gluteal nerve","The tibial nerve","The median nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The inferior gluteal nerve supplies the gluteus maximus, the powerful extensor of the thigh. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which muscle of mastication protrudes the mandible and helps open the mouth?","choices":["The temporalis muscle","The lateral pterygoid","The masseter muscle","The medial pterygoid"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lateral pterygoid protrudes the mandible and depresses it to help open the mouth. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How are the tongue muscles that change its shape, rather than its position, classified?","choices":["As intrinsic tongue muscles","As extrinsic tongue muscles","As muscles of mastication","As pharyngeal constrictors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Intrinsic tongue muscles lie entirely within the tongue and change its shape, while extrinsic muscles move its position. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"The scalene muscles of the lateral neck perform which action on the ribs?","choices":["They elevate the first two ribs","They depress the last two ribs","They rotate the eyeball","They close the eyelids"],"answer":0,"exp":"The anterior, middle, and posterior scalenes elevate the first two ribs and laterally flex the neck. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"The urogenital triangle is the anterior part of which body region?","choices":["The thoracic cavity","The cranial cavity","The perineum","The axilla"],"answer":2,"exp":"The urogenital triangle is the anterior portion of the perineum, containing the external genital and urethral structures. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which deep intrinsic back muscle extends the vertebral column and rotates the body toward the opposite side of the muscle?","choices":["Interspinales","Intertransversarii","Splenius capitis","Multifidus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Multifidus extends the vertebral column and rotates the body toward the side opposite the muscle, innervated by cervical, thoracic, and lumbar spinal nerves. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What are the main functions of the muscles of the perineum and pelvic diaphragm?","choices":["Rotate the head from side to side, flex the cervical spine, and stabilize the neck during swallowing","Extend the vertebral column, help maintain upright standing posture, and stabilize the trunk during lifting","Support pelvic organs, flex the joints of the sacrum/coccyx, and control movement of material through the urethra and anus","Elevate the ribs to assist forced inhalation during heavy exercise, exertion, or singing"],"answer":2,"exp":"The main functions of the muscles of the perineum and pelvic diaphragm are to support the organs of the pelvic cavity, flex the joints of the sacrum and coccyx, and control the movement of material through the urethra and anus. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"How many paired muscles of mastication move the mandible?","choices":["Four paired muscles","Two paired muscles","Six paired muscles","Eight paired muscles"],"answer":0,"exp":"There are four paired muscles of mastication: the masseter, temporalis, medial pterygoid, and lateral pterygoid. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which is the most superficial of the three flat abdominal muscles, its fibers running downward and medially?","choices":["The internal oblique","The transversus abdominis","The rectus abdominis","The external oblique"],"answer":3,"exp":"The external oblique is the most superficial flat abdominal muscle, with fibers directed inferomedially, like hands in the pockets. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which muscle draws the angle of the mouth upward and laterally, as in smiling?","choices":["The orbicularis oculi","The buccinator muscle","The platysma muscle","The zygomaticus major"],"answer":3,"exp":"The zygomaticus major runs from the zygomatic bone to the corner of the mouth and draws it up and out in smiling. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve supplies most of the extra-ocular muscles, except the lateral rectus and superior oblique?","choices":["The abducens nerve","The oculomotor nerve","The trochlear nerve","The trigeminal nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve three) supplies four of the six extra-ocular muscles, all except the lateral rectus and superior oblique. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"The perineum is divided into an anterior urogenital triangle and which posterior triangle?","choices":["The femoral triangle","The cervical triangle","The lumbar triangle","The anal triangle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The perineum is divided into the anterior urogenital triangle and the posterior anal triangle, which contains the anal opening. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which deeper group of intrinsic back muscles, lying beneath the erector spinae, includes the multifidus and rotates the spine?","choices":["The transversospinalis group","The extrinsic back group","The abdominal oblique group","The pharyngeal constrictor group"],"answer":0,"exp":"The transversospinalis group, including the semispinalis, multifidus, and rotatores, lies deep to the erector spinae and rotates and stabilizes the spine. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the neuromuscular junction?","choices":["The connective tissue sheath, known as the perimysium, that surrounds an individual muscle fascicle","The fibrous point of attachment where a tendon anchors skeletal muscle firmly onto the underlying bone","A structural junction that mechanically connects two adjacent skeletal muscles to one another","A specialized synapse where a motor neuron communicates with a skeletal muscle fiber to initiate contraction"],"answer":3,"exp":"The neuromuscular junction is a specialized synapse where a motor neuron communicates with a skeletal muscle fiber to initiate muscle contraction. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"The sternocleidomastoid receives its motor innervation from which cranial nerve?","choices":["The facial nerve","The accessory nerve","The hypoglossal nerve","The trigeminal nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sternocleidomastoid is supplied by the accessory nerve (cranial nerve eleven), which also supplies the trapezius. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which deep intrinsic back muscle produces lateral flexion of the vertebral column, distinct from the extension-focused actions of nearby deep muscles?","choices":["Rotatores muscle","Interspinales muscle","Multifidus muscle","Intertransversarii"],"answer":3,"exp":"Intertransversarii produces lateral flexion of the vertebral column. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"}]};
